#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093201} :activity/id #uuid "0c0d6ec4-01bc-5b0f-8b44-162a9c686020" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049605 :topic/id #uuid "03eacd71-e195-528e-bb29-8b23c4e95ba7" :topic/math-academy-id 971} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093201 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093475 :tutorial/content "Suppose that three events, $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3,$ exist within a sample space $\\mathcal S$ such that \n\n- $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3$ are *mutually exclusive*, and\n\n- the events $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3$ cover the *entire* sample space.\n\nWhen these two conditions are satisfied, we say that $\\mathcal S$ is the **disjoint union** of  $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3,$ and we write \n \n\n$$\n\\mathcal S = B_1 \\sqcup B_2 \\sqcup B_3.\n$$\n\nA diagram of this situation is shown below. Notice that $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3$ cover the entire diagram but do not overlap.\n\n![](images/f5/f5f9f04eb6affb9dca1f7d4e7d66d76c4d520cbf66db93db60639ce896fb19af.png)\n\n \n\nNow suppose that we wish to compute the probability of an event $A$ that lies within $\\mathcal S.$ Let's draw this event on our diagram and label its intersections with $B_1, B_2, B_3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ed/ed6568369a3af39cf1f6ecd5cb481de20d6c31bbce0fa1be54eb9ae0ab4e7f20.png)\n\n \n\nThe **law of total probability** can be deduced from this diagram. It states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A\\cap {B}_{1})+P(A\\cap {B}_{2})+P(A\\cap {B}_{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will only consider a disjoint union of $\\mathcal S$ consisting of three events. However, it should be noted that the law of total probability can be extended to an arbitrary number of events, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A\\cap {B}_{1})+P(A\\cap {B}_{2})+⋯ \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}P(A\\cap {B}_{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathcal S = B_1 \\sqcup B_2\\sqcup \\ldots$ is a disjoint union of $\\mathcal S.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d4103a1f-00a1-55d2-8e99-f1b230f8b348" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6295 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "34e9e254-fd36-5512-88cc-6abf808e561b" :step/math-academy-id 836329 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093206}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093202 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092314 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091925 :question/id #uuid "c690991e-6029-5067-b97a-67c5e62b7f47" :question/math-academy-id "e-10109"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4149b2fe-4243-595e-af7f-851b308f7eb1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050990}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515595}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability"} :step/id #uuid "92a781b8-11f6-5279-b328-89be09bb0155" :step/math-academy-id 836331 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093203}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093203 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093476 :tutorial/content "The law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A\\cap B_1) +  P(A\\cap B_2) +  P(A\\cap B_3),\n$$\n\nwhere $B_1\\sqcup B_2\\sqcup B_3$ is a disjoint union of the sample space $\\mathcal S.$\n\nOften, it's more helpful to express this law using conditional probabilities. \n\nFrom the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cap {B}_{1}) & =P(A|{B}_{1})P({B}_{1}) \\\\ P(A\\cap {B}_{2}) & =P(A|{B}_{2})P({B}_{2}) \\\\ P(A\\cap {B}_{3}) & =P(A|{B}_{3})P({B}_{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above form into the law of total probability, we get\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A|B_1)P(B_1)+  P(A|B_2)P(B_2) +  P(A|B_3)P(B_3).\n$$\n\nLet's see an example of how to apply this." :tutorial/id #uuid "344e2752-b37e-517e-8057-b24f42e51566" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6296 :tutorial/title "The Law of Total Probability in Terms of Conditional Probability"} :step/id #uuid "a17de8a8-a153-5e7d-be7b-53093b1da395" :step/math-academy-id 836334 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093204}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093204 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092315 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091926 :question/id #uuid "1104f073-7f90-51ce-9e95-aae0935ca852" :question/math-academy-id "e-10110"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96f54294-8bb4-5338-969b-76c8d1536daa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551319}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability in Terms of Conditional Probability"} :step/id #uuid "80843a9c-ed90-5e4d-9462-e0eb6c40068c" :step/math-academy-id 836335 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093205}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093205 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092316 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091927 :question/id #uuid "ae39bb45-ac10-562a-bac6-4f192c5f0aed" :question/math-academy-id "e-10111"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0ebe700-63af-57d4-ae8f-9e09dac5cf76" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551304}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Using the Law of Total Probability"} :step/id #uuid "b7b7a4f5-8ab3-527e-b01f-844c9aeadcbe" :step/math-academy-id 836338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093206 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093477 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a particular garden is divided into three small plots. There are $10$ roses in the first plot, of which $5$ are red, $12$ roses in the second plot, of which $6$ are red, and $14$ roses in the third plot, of which $4$ are red. What is the probability that a randomly selected rose from the garden is red?\n\nLet $A$ be the event that a chosen rose is red, and \n\n- let $B_1$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the first plot,\n\n- let $B_2$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the second plot,\n\n- let $B_3$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the third plot.\n\nNotice that the disjoint union\n\n$$\nB_1\\sqcup B_2\\sqcup B_3\n$$\n\ncovers the entire sample space, and that there are a total of \n\n$$\n10+12+14=36\n$$\n\nroses in the garden. \n\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_1),$ we take the number of red roses in the first plot $(5)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(A \\cap B_1) = \\dfrac{5}{36}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_2),$ we take the number of red roses in the second plot $(6)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(A \\cap B_2) = \\dfrac{6}{36}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_3),$ we take the number of red roses in the third plot $(4)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(A \\cap B_3) = \\dfrac{4}{36}.\n$$\n\nSo, to calculate the probability that a randomly chosen rose is red, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A\\cap {B}_{1})+P(A\\cap {B}_{2})+P(A\\cap {B}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{36}+\\frac{6}{36}+\\frac{4}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNote the following: \n\n- You may have noticed that there is an easier way to solve this problem. That is, to sum the total number of red roses $(5+6+4 = 15)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(10+12+14 = 36).$ However, you're encouraged to work with the law of total probability as it's essential to get used to the concept.\n\n- A little later in this lesson, we'll work with a version of the law of total probability that isn't intuitive. Understanding the law of total probability in its simplest form will help when things get trickier.\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "9b6238d0-0917-5d62-b878-311127fce7fe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6446 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "1477f9c6-f997-5b76-9d95-747c31eb50a4" :step/math-academy-id 836330 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093202}}] :activity/title "Extending the Law of Total Probability" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092453}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492953} :activity/id #uuid "cb177df5-72c8-5d78-b659-9b78f111f63f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049605 :topic/id #uuid "03eacd71-e195-528e-bb29-8b23c4e95ba7" :topic/math-academy-id 971} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492947 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0145d796-eb28-54a8-95f3-28e7aefa0081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492225} {:answer/id #uuid "45356b43-9eb8-5668-952a-155b42bb6eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492353} {:answer/id #uuid "693adf90-92ac-557a-8ea3-07804f12f595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492427} {:answer/id #uuid "78ae7c7a-fba7-5249-b0f2-20cf7941b79c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492461} {:answer/id #uuid "b321f881-cce5-5d65-bf3b-f929adf85f17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492574}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492225} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea73b3dd-bf88-5f7b-9b97-f1ed24555540" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e343068f-45c7-52dd-ac5c-c29d4a7a5cbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-143559" :question/problem "A company manufactures electronic components. The components are produced by three machines, $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$ In a particular random sample, $31$ of the components were produced by machine $A,$ $34$ by machine $B,$ and $35$ by machine $C.$ On inspecting the sample for defective components, it was found that $2$ defective components were produced by machine $A,$ $1$  by machine $B,$ and $2$  by machine $C.$ Based on this data, what is the probability that a randomly selected component from the sample is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that the chosen component is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the  component was produced  by  machine $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the  component was produced  by  machine $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the  component was produced  by  machine $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component from the sample is defective, we use the law of total probability:  \n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S\\cap A)+P(S\\cap B)+P(S\\cap C)\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that there is a total of \n\n$$\n31+34+35=100\n$$\n\ncomponents in the sample.\n\n- To calculate $P(S\\cap A),$ we take the number defective components produced by machine $A$ $(2)$ and divide by the total number of components produced $(100).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap A)=\\frac{2}{100}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(S\\cap B),$ we take the number defective components produced by machine $B$ $(1)$ and divide by the total number of components produced  $(100).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(S\\cap B)=\\frac{1}{100}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(S\\cap C),$ we take the number defective components produced by machine $C$ $(2)$ and divide by the total number of components produced  $(100).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(S\\cap C)=\\frac{2}{100}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly component is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S\\cap A)+P(S\\cap B)+P(S\\cap C) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{100}+\\frac{1}{100}+\\frac{2}{100} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{100} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "48161813-f84d-504c-b8c4-db0136c2de37" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492977}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492953 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9eae800-1856-54ad-a17f-88cd4e179f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089526} {:answer/id #uuid "b55beceb-c89e-57b6-9865-3a9a31ea6753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089527} {:answer/id #uuid "bea4d391-e2d8-5f7d-b816-23e4107fc373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089528} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb4624d-ab46-57be-a2fe-2083f5c47ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089529} {:answer/id #uuid "de8f08a7-ee40-5001-9780-39a8ddcc4bed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089530}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186089530} :answer-field/id #uuid "e682799a-c8c0-5ebd-a2b3-68091a66eb2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d262f09b-86ba-5f7c-9ab7-53a1fc7fd15f" :question/math-academy-id "q-143530" :question/problem "A math teacher splits her calculus class into three groups such that $35%$ of all the students are in Group A, $35%$ are in Group B, and $30%$ are in Group C. If $20%$ of the students in Group A obtained the top grade, $10%$ in Group B obtained the top grade, and the probability that a randomly selected student from any group obtained the top grade is $0.255,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected student from Group C obtained the top grade?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $T$ be the event that a chosen student obtained the top grade, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the student is from Group A,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the student is from Group B,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the student is from Group С.\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student obtained the top grade, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(T\\cap A)+P(T\\cap B)+P(T\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|B)P(B)+P(T|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $35%$ of students are in Group A, $35%$ are in Group B, and $30%$ are in Group C, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.35,\\,P(B)=0.35,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of the students in Group A, and $10%$ in Group B obtained the top grade, we have\n\n$$\nP(T|A)=0.2,\\,P(T|B)=0.1\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected student from any group obtained the top grade is $0.255,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=0.255.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(T|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|B)P(B)+P(T|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.255 & =(0.2)(0.35)+(0.1)(0.35)+P(T|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.255 & =0.07+0.035+0.3P(T|C) \\\\ 0.3P(T|C) & =0.15 \\\\ P(T|C) & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected student from Group C obtained the top grade is $0.50.$"} :step/id #uuid "64fd50f2-7184-51d1-b27f-040fb0e0ea7c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492947}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492970 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b399609-7f79-5173-9ee0-8e611fd769c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.068" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492398} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd79dd8-46de-5029-9f80-7bb504d1b873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.088" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492408} {:answer/id #uuid "837910a1-2e2b-5bf0-9265-dbc58e68c246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.072" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492483} {:answer/id #uuid "e355882d-ac3b-5bff-9a6a-e73a0f92e528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.092" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492659} {:answer/id #uuid "ed962bd1-71d7-545c-8b5c-392d81210bc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.062" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492675}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492408} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0a0ca37-d77c-51fb-b507-a74b429aeacd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aa34c10c-c85d-5965-879f-040a2dcc2a58" :question/math-academy-id "q-143759" :question/problem "Ann has three email accounts. In the first account, she receives $40%$ of her emails, of which $10%$ are spam. In the second account, she receives $35%$ of her emails, of which $8%$ are spam. And in the third account, she receives $25%$ of her emails, of which $8%$ are spam. Based on this data, what is the probability that a randomly selected email from any of Ann's accounts is spam?" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that the selected email is spam, and\n\n- let ${A}_{1}$ be the event that the email was received in the first account,\n\n- let ${A}_{2}$ be the event that the email was received in the second account,\n\n- let ${A}_{3}$ be the event that the email was received in the third account.\n\nThen the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected email is spam, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S\\cap {A}_{1})+P(S\\cap {A}_{2})+P(S\\cap {A}_{3}) \\\\  & =P(S|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(S|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(S|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since Ann receives $40%$ of her emails in the first account,  $35%$ in the second account, and $25%$ in the third account, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})=0.4,\\,P({A}_{2})=0.35,\\,P({A}_{3})=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of the emails received in the first account, $8%$ of the emails received in the first account, and $8%$ of the emails received in the third account are spam, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|{A}_{1})=0.1,\\,P(S|{A}_{2})=0.08\\,P(S|{A}_{3})=0.08.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected email from any of Ann's accounts is spam is   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(S|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(S|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3}) \\\\  & =(0.1)(0.4)+(0.08)(0.35)+(0.08)(0.25) \\\\  & =0.04+0.028+0.02 \\\\  & =0.088.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "90883805-26ab-5fc5-bb58-296a7633b093" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492981}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492977 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24a9eb73-0014-5463-b63e-0a940d9e2582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.036" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492292} {:answer/id #uuid "5282849f-2a3c-5f32-b6be-060b8370b7f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.041" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492374} {:answer/id #uuid "5460928c-e102-597f-9db9-80c61e0a7e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.052" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492377} {:answer/id #uuid "7d996b1c-82f7-598f-be8d-1b498be533e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.049" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492469} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c243cf-613b-5eb8-9ed8-a7b18e1d6ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.047" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492695}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492374} :answer-field/id #uuid "03277a1a-6eea-5e2a-9857-810f8df632af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a98874b4-d2c1-50c9-83d1-a1b2a1981db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-143721" :question/problem "Three factories produce the same tool for the market. Factory $A$ produces $30%$ of the tools, of which $2%$ are defective. Factory $B$ produces $34%$ of the tools, of which $5%$ are defective, and factory $C$ produces $36%$ of the tools, of which $5%$ are defective. What is the probability that a randomly selected tool is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected tool is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the tool was produced by factory $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the tool was produced by factory $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the tool was produced by factory $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected tool is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since factory $A$ produces $30%$ of the tools, factory $B$ produces $34%$ of the tools, and factory $C$ produces $36%$ of the tools, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.3,\\,P(B)=0.34,\\,P(C)=0.36.\n$$\n\n- Since $2%$ of the tools produced by factory $A$, $5%$ of the tools produced by factory $B$, and $5%$ of the tools produced by factory $C,$ are defective, we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.02,\\,P(D|B)=0.05,\\,P(D|C)=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected tool is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.02)(0.3)+(0.05)(0.34)+(0.05)(0.36) \\\\  & =0.006+0.017+0.018 \\\\  & =0.041.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a2414de0-21e9-54d6-9573-da1fa9464bbd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492970}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492981 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1443b175-b2e6-58e7-9266-46bb002bf1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492252} {:answer/id #uuid "731bab39-0cc7-53b5-8a98-4114c8993795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492446} {:answer/id #uuid "8aefa18a-70e2-59eb-a0e8-c4294cece2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492493} {:answer/id #uuid "92f9cac9-b592-5ea1-b5f0-997e20fedd02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492503} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe75c90-a8f8-5625-9747-315993606532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492704}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492493} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c481d0c-6bec-5a2b-817b-51131184fbf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "33d92120-f62e-56e8-8525-dfbf23370577" :question/math-academy-id "q-143978" :question/problem "In a hardware store, there are three boxes containing light bulbs. Box $A$ contains $15$ bulbs, of which $2$ are defective, and box $B$ contains $10$ bulbs, of which $1$ is defective. A box is selected at random, and a bulb is randomly chosen from the box. If the probability that the selected bulb is defective is $\\frac{2}{15},$ what is the probability that a randomly selected bulb from box $C$ is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event of selecting a defective bulb at random from one of the three boxes, and   \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the bulb is from box $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the bulb is  from box $B$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the bulb is from box $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space. \n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a defective bulb, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation. \n\n- Since a box is chosen at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $2$ defective bulbs out of $15$  in box $A$ and  $1$ defective bulb out of $10$ in box $B,$  we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=\\frac{2}{15},\\,P(D|B)=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected bulb is defective is $\\frac{2}{15},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=\\frac{2}{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{2}{15} & =(\\frac{2}{15})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{10})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(D|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{2}{15} & =\\frac{2}{45}+\\frac{1}{30}+\\frac{1}{3}P(D|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}P(D|C) & =\\frac{1}{18} \\\\ P(D|C) & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected bulb from box $C$ is defective is $\\frac{1}{6}.$"} :step/id #uuid "a66ad3c1-21d4-525d-b81a-6c389b90b1cb"}] :activity/title "Extending the Law of Total Probability" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492215}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551469} :activity/id #uuid "22ea1569-895b-50ca-99d6-870b4789bc76" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049605 :topic/id #uuid "03eacd71-e195-528e-bb29-8b23c4e95ba7" :topic/math-academy-id 971} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551358 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63016c40-26b8-54f1-af02-fe4df536e028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.044" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436173} {:answer/id #uuid "9e6154ce-f843-5fdc-a8b0-5dfb33599b4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.058" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436185} {:answer/id #uuid "cc67574e-605f-521d-93f0-a43b7eb005d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.541" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436195} {:answer/id #uuid "e6bab3ee-bcc0-51a4-967f-ae1ffb30e505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.056" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436204} {:answer/id #uuid "efcedd14-6e3b-5303-8273-c5f15c5397fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.054" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436206}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436173} :answer-field/id #uuid "efcaebab-808b-586a-b686-8d0602967092" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "296a9d4d-087e-5552-b718-aa436d5fbac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143748" :question/problem "A company produces replacement batteries for a particular cell phone model at three locations. Location $A$ produces $30%$ of the batteries, of which $3%$ are defective. Location $B$ produces $38%$ of the batteries, of which $5%$ are defective. And location $C$  produces $32%$ of the batteries, of which also $5%$ are defective. What is the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that the battery is defective, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the battery was produced at location $A,$\n\n- let $B$  be the event that the battery was produced at location $B,$\n\n- let $C$  be the event that the battery was produced at location $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since  location $A$ produces $30%$ of the batteries,  location $B$ produces $38%$ of the batteries, and location $C$ produces $32%$ of the batteries, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.3,\\,P(B)=0.38,\\,P(C)=0.32.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of the batteries produced at location $A$, $5%$ of the batteries produced at location $B$, and $5%$ of the batteries produced at  location $C,$ are defective, we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.03,\\,P(D|B)=0.05,\\,P(D|C)=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.03)(0.3)+(0.05)(0.38)+(0.05)(0.32) \\\\  & =0.009+0.019+0.016 \\\\  & =0.044.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "015fd7cc-296c-527b-8d93-783a3144a040" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551390}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551383 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551304 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18462a6e-e5aa-5da9-9169-1897af9ddfc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "74%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550421} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd5ac84-c3f0-5b1e-9dc6-1b0238589e1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "71%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550549} {:answer/id #uuid "88aaed32-cbc4-5fb1-b688-7cc95b6f62b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "59%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550715} {:answer/id #uuid "956129cb-062c-52cb-8b95-1feac6e6425c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "79%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550743} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5f37d0-df7f-5e60-8207-efacf2cdc514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550924}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550549} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2414de2-0b71-5f21-ba04-5ccbaa91ec84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "87509e91-72b0-5f1f-83b2-ad41631e8459" :question/math-academy-id "q-144037" :question/problem "A cattle rancher has three ranches, where $34%$ of the rancher's cows are on ranch $A$, $36%$ are on ranch $B,$ and $30%$ on ranch $C.$ If $25%$ of all cows on ranch $A$ and $20%$ of all cows on ranch $B$ are dairy cows, and the probability that a randomly selected cow from any of the three ranches is a dairy cow is $37%,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected cow from ranch $C$ is a dairy cow?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a selected cow is a dairy cow, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the cow is from ranch $A$,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the cow is from ranch $B$,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the cow is from ranch $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that the randomly selected cow is a dairy cow, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation. \n\n- Since $34%$ of all the rancher's cows are on ranch $A$, $36%$ are on ranch $B,$ and $30%$ are on ranch $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.34,\\,P(B)=0.36,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $25%$ of all cows on ranch $A$ and $20%$ of all cows on ranch $B$ are dairy cows, we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.25,\\,P(D|B)=0.2.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected cow from any of the three ranches is  a dairy cow is $37%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.37.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.37 & =(0.25)(0.34)+(0.2)(0.36)+P(D|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.37 & =0.085+0.072+0.3P(D|C) \\\\ 0.3P(D|C) & =0.213 \\\\ P(D|C) & =0.71\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected cow from ranch $C$ is a dairy cow is $71%.$"} :step/id #uuid "330a5393-8793-5481-819f-7f472b79e0cb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a66e4d-d36b-5589-9e76-8d2fc1124283" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.525" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436146} {:answer/id #uuid "32133a22-82bc-5a7c-9eac-727b8484e3b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.515" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436154} {:answer/id #uuid "357f442f-a27d-5c2b-8c03-4173f9ed1135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.555" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436158} {:answer/id #uuid "35872c71-32ca-5629-8a73-9868fcb5d5a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.625" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436159} {:answer/id #uuid "82363ae0-ea2c-5ea2-bd36-f1a97cf797bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.575" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436178}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436178} :answer-field/id #uuid "e132532a-be97-5775-bb17-2406d8caeef2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7c588063-b6d3-5525-ae01-ed797a4d9cc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143753" :question/problem "In a gas station, $40%$ of the clients buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ buy Supreme gasoline. If $65%$ of the clients that purchase Regular gasoline paid with a credit card, $50%$ of the clients that buy Premium gasoline paid with a credit card, and $60%$ of the clients that buy Supreme gasoline paid with a credit card,  what is the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card?" :question/worked-solution "Let  $C$ be the event that the client paid with a credit card, and\n\n- let $R$ be the event that the client bought Regular gasoline,\n\n- let $Q$  be the event that the client bought Premium gasoline,\n\n- let $S$ be the event that the client bought Supreme gasoline.\n\nThen the disjoint union $R⊔Q⊔S$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C\\cap R)+P(C\\cap Q)+P(C\\cap S) \\\\  & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of the clients buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ of the clients buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ of the clients buy Supreme gasoline, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.4,\\,P(Q)=0.45,\\,P(S)=0.15.\n$$\n\n- Since $65%$ of the clients that buy Regular gasoline, $50%$ of the clients that buy Premium gasoline, and $60%$ of the clients that buy Supreme gasoline,  paid with a credit card, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.65,\\,P(C|Q)=0.5,\\,P(C|S)=0.6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S) \\\\  & =(0.65)(0.4)+(0.5)(0.45)+(0.6)(0.15) \\\\  & =0.26+0.225+0.009 \\\\  & =0.575.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3ab20fd2-6a65-50a2-902a-224e12d123dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551437}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551437 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49d67ba6-1b1f-58fa-a1c0-e61e944c3560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.044" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436163} {:answer/id #uuid "82276f72-0877-5798-8fe1-76ed94a97b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436177} {:answer/id #uuid "9482cc37-4f52-55f0-820b-76e7d8d26527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.038" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436182} {:answer/id #uuid "e4fcf1de-ebb1-592d-9f04-b9c050c3551b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0052" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436203} {:answer/id #uuid "ea852479-a868-5154-ba52-8c97031f5080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436205}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436205} :answer-field/id #uuid "e674252e-02a4-53ae-adde-553bbd0c47c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "199cacb7-9e7a-5d07-af35-fc181af78213" :question/math-academy-id "q-143872" :question/problem "A company manufactures electronic components. Of the component population, $45%$ are produced by machine $A,$  $25%$ by machine $B,$ and $30%$ by machine $C.$ If $5%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ and $3%$ of those produced by machine $B$ are defective, and the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is $0.042,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $C$ is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected component is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation. \n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $45%$ of the components, machine $B$ produces $25%$ of the components, and machine $C$ produces $30%$ of the components, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.45,\\,P(B)=0.25,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $5%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ and $3%$ of the components produced by machine $B$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.05,\\,P(D|B)=0.03\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is $0.042,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.042.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.042 & =(0.05)(0.45)+(0.03)(0.25)+P(D|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.042 & =0.0225+0.0075+0.3P(D|C) \\\\ 0.3P(D|C) & =0.012 \\\\ P(D|C) & =0.04\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $C$ is defective is $0.04.$"} :step/id #uuid "b031a733-e72b-5ee4-a3be-0e48bbdb6dfb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551383}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551469 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551319 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2091824b-6f43-593c-84fe-25de29e7e4e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550441} {:answer/id #uuid "4da788ef-fc41-5934-85c7-6c54e9d66986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{63}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550552} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9ac3d1-fd3a-5663-99f5-0b6e86c9d756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{90}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550690} {:answer/id #uuid "8a76f1ca-01f8-5917-8e75-64c4de462b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{165}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550717} {:answer/id #uuid "8e788705-609a-5a7a-af02-0595198b67ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{113}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550731}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550690} :answer-field/id #uuid "be770eee-142d-5138-82b0-d774ea34700b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a3aefa5f-7730-50af-bdda-78228d7a1e41" :question/math-academy-id "q-143572" :question/problem "In a hardware store, there are three boxes containing light bulbs. Box $A$ contains $15$ bulbs, of which $2$ are defective. Box $B$ contains $10$ bulbs, of which $1$ is defective. And box $C$ contains $10$ light bulbs, of which $2$ are defective. A box is selected at random, and a bulb is randomly selected from the box. Find the probability that the bulb is defective." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event of selecting a defective bulb, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the bulb is from box $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the bulb is from box $B$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the bulb is from box $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space. \n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a defective bulb at random from one of the three boxes, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a box is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $2$ defective bulbs out of $15$  in box $A,$ $1$ defective bulb out of $10$ in box $B,$ and $2$  defective bulbs out of $10$ bulbs in box $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=\\frac{2}{15},\\,P(D|B)=\\frac{1}{10},\\,P(D|C)=\\frac{2}{10}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability of selecting a defective bulb at random from one of the three boxes is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{15})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{10})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{2}{10})(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{45}+\\frac{1}{30}+\\frac{2}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{90}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f9f7759e-a162-5e5d-8af0-e5c76d19e07c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551358}}] :activity/title "Extending the Law of Total Probability" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550344}]}
